<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Leo I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 391–<a href="/years/461">461</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Leo the Great, Leo Magnus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope Leo I, also known as Leo the Great, was a Roman aristocrat who served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from 29 September 440 until his death in 461. He developed ideas of papal authority, grounding them in the apostolic succession of the bishop of Rome from Saint Peter. In 452 he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila the Hun</a> and persuaded him to turn back from his invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, though scholars debate how large a part his authority played. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-chalcedon">Council of Chalcedon</a> his Tome of Leo set out the union of Christ's divine and human natures in one person. Pope <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedict-xiv">Benedict XIV</a> named him a Doctor of the Church.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-leo-i.jpg" alt="Pope Leo I" width="330" height="517" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francisco Herrera the Younger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herrera_mozo_San_León_magno_Lienzo._Óvalo._164_x_105_cm._Museo_del_Prado.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Christian hagiography made them antagonists of saints: Attila turned back by <b>Pope Leo I</b> and the apostles, Saint Ursula and her virgins killed at Cologne.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hunnic-empire">Hunnic Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
